                <pb facs="0362" n="280" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg280"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.612/47: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kellogg</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-02-02T16:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 2,
                                1925—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received February 2—2:55
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>47. Your 52, January 30, 6 p.m. I personally presented a strong note to
                    Chamberlain on the potato shipments and verbally explained <pb facs="0363"
                        n="281" xml:id="pg281"/> the whole situation, urging him to raise the
                    embargo in view of the certificate Department proposed. He promised to take the
                    matter up [with] the Minister of Agriculture at once as the matter of shipments
                    from Canada was under investigation, but made no definite promises.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
